Here’s a simple backend upload handler that will use your upload preset and asset folder.
const cloudinary = require('cloudinary').v2;

cloudinary.config({
  cloud_name: 'your-cloud-name',
  api_key: 'your-api-key',
  api_secret: 'your-api-secret'
});

const uploadImage = async (req, res) => {
  const file = req.body.file; // or however you receive the file in your request
  const uploadResult = await cloudinary.uploader.upload(file, {
    upload_preset: 'Krieta',
    folder: 'Krieta/uploads'
  });
  res.json(uploadResult);
};

module.exports = { uploadImage };

If you’re uploading from the frontend (not a serverless function), you can also send the file as FormData and include upload_preset in the request body.
If you’re using a signed upload preset, you still send upload_preset in the upload request, but you also need to sign the request with a signature (and a timestamp).
So your backend upload call should include:
upload_preset: 'Krieta'
signature (generated from your API secret + timestamp + other params)
timestamp
The asset_folder (like Krieta/uploads) is set in the preset itself, so you don’t need to pass it in the upload call for signed presets.
For a signed upload preset in Node.js, your backend should generate a signature, then your mobile app sends upload_preset, api_key, timestamp, and signature in the upload request (the SDK can do this automatically if you use it).
Backend (signature endpoint):
const cloudinary = require('cloudinary').v2;

const getUploadSignature = (req, res) => {
  const timestamp = Math.round(new Date().getTime() / 1000);
  const signature = cloudinary.utils.api_sign_request(
    { timestamp, upload_preset: 'Krieta' },
    process.env.CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET
  );
  res.json({ signature, timestamp, apiKey: process.env.CLOUDINARY_API_KEY });
};
Your mobile app then uploads using upload_preset: 'Krieta' plus the returned signature and timestamp (and api_key).
